course: dictionary
lesson: 8511
title: Rare and made-up words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words look strange because they are rare, very old, or made up by a writer on purpose. Context helps you work them out. In «The Elephant's Child», Kipling writes «'satiable curtiosity», and the words around it give you the clues.
explain: The sentence says the young elephant was full of this curiosity and asked everybody questions, so «'satiable curtiosity» must mean endless curiosity. The bit in brackets, «(endless curiosity)», confirms it. Try your meaning in the sentence: does «full of endless curiosity» still make sense? It does.
explain: Satiable on its own is a rare word. It means able to be satisfied or filled. Kipling's baby-talk is short for «insatiable», the real word for never satisfied. Knowing the real word helps you understand the made-up spelling.
fact: Satiable means able to be satisfied or filled.
fact: «Insatiable» means never satisfied, so you can never get enough.
fact: «'Satiable curtiosity» is Kipling's baby-talk for «insatiable curiosity».
fact: Kipling spells words wrongly on purpose, to sound like a small child.
fact: Context clues can help you understand a rare word, even if you have never seen it before.
exercise: «The Elephant's Child was full of 'satiable curtiosity.» What does this mean in the story? | *Endless curiosity, wanting to know everything | Sleepy and quiet all day | Not very interested in anything | A nose as big as a boot
exercise: Which real word means never satisfied, so you can never get enough? | Sleepy | *Insatiable | Curious | Satiable
exercise: «All his aunts and uncles spanked him for it.» Why were they cross with the Elephant's Child? | *Because he asked everybody so many questions | Because he was very good and quiet | Because he was lost in the forest | Because he won a race
test: «'Satiable» is a rare word. What does it mean on its own? | Full of sadness and tears | Very loud and noisy | *Able to be satisfied or filled | Made of glass and easily broken
test: Kipling writes «'satiable curtiosity». Which real word is it short for? | Satisfied | *Insatiable | Curious | Curtains
exam: Why does Kipling spell curiosity as «curtiosity» in «The Elephant's Child»? | To hide the meaning of the story | To make a spelling test for his readers | To show that he cannot spell | *To sound like a small child using baby-talk
